1
00:25:37,723 --> 00:25:40,089
Here you are, sir. Main level, please.

2
00:25:41,060 --> 00:25:42,892
Right. See you on the way back.

3
00:25:42,962 --> 00:25:44,191
-Bye.
-Bye.

4
00:25:45,699 --> 00:25:47,222
-Good morning, sir.
-Good morning.

5
00:25:47,299 --> 00:25:49,268
We haven't seen you up here
for a long time.

6
00:25:49,336 --> 00:25:50,701
No. Very nice to see you again.

7
00:25:50,769 --> 00:25:53,603
-Did you have a pleasant flight, sir?
-Yes, very nice, thanks.

8
00:25:53,673 --> 00:25:56,869
I think Mr. Miller of Station Security
is supposed to be meeting me.

9
00:25:56,942 --> 00:25:57,967
May I call him for you?

10
00:25:58,044 --> 00:25:59,203
Would you, please? Here he is!

11
00:25:59,278 --> 00:26:01,247
-Hello, Dr. Floyd.
-Hello, Miller! How are you?

12
00:26:01,314 --> 00:26:03,544
-Sorry I'm late.
-That's all right. You look great.

13
00:26:03,616 --> 00:26:05,448
Thank you. It's nice to have you back.

14
00:26:05,518 --> 00:26:07,884
-Did you have a good flight?
-Very nice indeed.

15
00:26:07,953 --> 00:26:09,421
-Well, shall we go through?
-Okay.

16
00:26:09,489 --> 00:26:10,955
Will you please use Number 17?

17
00:26:11,023 --> 00:26:12,787
-Thank you, Miss Turner.
-Thank you.

18
00:26:12,858 --> 00:26:13,916
This way.

19
00:26:19,266 --> 00:26:21,529
Welcome to Voice Print Identification.

20
00:26:21,768 --> 00:26:23,701
When you see the red light go on...

21
00:26:23,769 --> 00:26:26,238
...would you please state
in the following order:

22
00:26:26,306 --> 00:26:30,140
your destination, your nationality
and your full name.

23
00:26:30,609 --> 00:26:33,442
Surname first, Christian name and initial.

24
00:26:35,181 --> 00:26:39,140
Moon. American. Floyd, Heywood R.

25
00:26:41,855 --> 00:26:45,790
Thank you. You are cleared through
Voice Print Identification.

26
00:26:46,292 --> 00:26:47,419
Thank you.

27
00:26:54,567 --> 00:26:56,228
Have I got time for breakfast?

28
00:26:56,303 --> 00:26:57,769
I think we can manage that.

29
00:26:57,836 --> 00:26:59,065
How long have I got?

30
00:26:59,138 --> 00:27:01,767
Your flight leaves
in an hour and ten minutes.

31
00:27:01,840 --> 00:27:04,809
I've reserved a table for you
in the Earthlight Room.

32
00:27:05,478 --> 00:27:09,005
It's been about seven or eight months
since you were here last, isn't it?

33
00:27:09,082 --> 00:27:10,777
Let's see. Yeah, about eight months.

34
00:27:10,849 --> 00:27:14,148
I suppose you saw the work on
the new section when you came in here?

35
00:27:14,220 --> 00:27:16,551
-Yes, coming along great, huh?
-Yeah, it's fine.

36
00:27:16,623 --> 00:27:17,714
Oh, wait a minute!

37
00:27:17,790 --> 00:27:21,227
I've got a few calls to make.
I'll meet you in the restaurant.

38
00:27:39,378 --> 00:27:42,211
Will Mr. Travers please contact
the Met Office?

39
00:27:43,482 --> 00:27:46,384
Will Mr. Travers please contact
the Met Office?

40
00:27:51,523 --> 00:27:52,354
Yes?

41
00:27:53,759 --> 00:27:55,522
-How are you, squirt?
-All right.

42
00:27:55,595 --> 00:27:57,061
What are you doin'?

43
00:27:58,664 --> 00:27:59,632
Playing.

44
00:27:59,932 --> 00:28:01,161
Where's Mommy?

45
00:28:01,667 --> 00:28:03,067
Gone to shopping.

46
00:28:03,369 --> 00:28:05,701
-Well, who's taking care of ya?
-Rachel.

47
00:28:06,038 --> 00:28:07,700
May I speak to Rachel, please?

48
00:28:07,773 --> 00:28:09,537
She's gone to the bathroom.

49
00:28:09,675 --> 00:28:12,109
Are you coming to my party tomorrow?

50
00:28:12,644 --> 00:28:14,839
I'm sorry, sweetheart, but I can't.

51
00:28:15,515 --> 00:28:16,573
Why not?

52
00:28:16,915 --> 00:28:19,349
Well, you know, Daddy's traveling.

53
00:28:19,652 --> 00:28:22,018
Very sorry about it, but I just can't!

54
00:28:23,455 --> 00:28:26,253
I'm gonna send you
a very nice present, though.

55
00:28:27,961 --> 00:28:28,984
All right.

56
00:28:29,061 --> 00:28:31,394
-Anything special that you want?
- Yes.

57
00:28:31,564 --> 00:28:32,587
What?

58
00:28:32,898 --> 00:28:34,025
A telephone.

59
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:36,991
We've got lots of telephones already.

60
00:28:37,069 --> 00:28:39,834
Can't you think of anything else
you want for your birthday?

61
00:28:39,905 --> 00:28:41,373
Something very special?

62
00:28:41,907 --> 00:28:43,465
- Yes.
-What?

63
00:28:43,742 --> 00:28:44,903
A bush baby.

64
00:28:44,978 --> 00:28:47,502
A bush baby?
Well, we'll have to see about that.

65
00:28:47,579 --> 00:28:49,548
I want you to tell Mommy
something for me.

66
00:28:49,615 --> 00:28:51,516
-Will you remember?
- Yes.

67
00:28:51,851 --> 00:28:54,318
Well, tell Mommy that I telephoned. Okay?

68
00:28:55,454 --> 00:28:56,217
Yes.

69
00:28:56,288 --> 00:28:58,951
And that I'll try
to telephone again tomorrow.

70
00:28:59,392 --> 00:29:01,189
Now, will you tell her that?

71
00:29:02,228 --> 00:29:03,057
Yes.

72
00:29:03,229 --> 00:29:06,163
Okay, sweetheart.
Now, have a nice birthday tomorrow.

73
00:29:06,231 --> 00:29:07,289
All right.

74
00:29:07,634 --> 00:29:10,467
And have a nice birthday party
tomorrow, too, huh?

75
00:29:11,438 --> 00:29:12,529
All right.

76
00:29:12,672 --> 00:29:15,538
Okay, now, take care and be a good girl,
won't you?

77
00:29:15,607 --> 00:29:16,939
All right. Bye-bye!

78
00:29:17,009 --> 00:29:19,569
Bye-bye! Happy birthday.

79
00:29:33,259 --> 00:29:35,453
Elena, how nice to see you again.

80
00:29:35,528 --> 00:29:37,621
What a wonderful surprise
to meet you here!

81
00:29:37,696 --> 00:29:40,757
-You're looking wonderful.
-Thank you. You're looking well, too.

82
00:29:40,834 --> 00:29:43,028
This is my good friend, Dr. Heywood Floyd.

83
00:29:43,102 --> 00:29:45,569
-I'd like you to meet Dr. Kalinan.
-How do you do?

84
00:29:45,637 --> 00:29:47,765
-Dr. Stretyneva.
-How do you do?

85
00:29:47,840 --> 00:29:49,705
And this is Dr. Andrei Smyslov.

86
00:29:49,776 --> 00:29:52,403
How do you do? I've heard a lot about you.

87
00:29:52,479 --> 00:29:53,945
Won't you sit down?

88
00:29:54,346 --> 00:29:56,211
-Well, we--
-No, no, please.

89
00:29:56,749 --> 00:29:58,215
Well, thank you.

90
00:29:58,484 --> 00:30:01,510
-Would you like a drink, Doctor?
-No, thank you.

91
00:30:01,587 --> 00:30:04,886
I haven't had breakfast yet.
Someone's meeting me in the restaurant.

92
00:30:04,958 --> 00:30:07,825
I'll just sit with you a few minutes
and then I must be off.

93
00:30:07,894 --> 00:30:10,920
-Are you quite sure?
-I'm quite sure, thank you.

94
00:30:11,564 --> 00:30:13,691
-And how's Gregor?
-He's fine.

95
00:30:14,032 --> 00:30:17,400
He's been doing some underwater research
in the Baltic so...

96
00:30:17,569 --> 00:30:21,665
...I'm afraid we don't get a chance
to see very much of each other these days.

97
00:30:21,740 --> 00:30:23,834
When you do see him,
give him my regards, huh?

98
00:30:23,910 --> 00:30:25,376
Yes, of course.

99
00:30:25,778 --> 00:30:28,337
Well, where are you all off to?
Up or down?

100
00:30:28,480 --> 00:30:30,038
Oh, we're going home.

101
00:30:30,182 --> 00:30:34,414
We have just spent three months
calibrating the new antennae at Tchalinko.

102
00:30:35,288 --> 00:30:38,121
-And what about you?
-I'm just on my way up to Clavius.

103
00:30:38,191 --> 00:30:39,589
Oh, are you?

104
00:30:42,194 --> 00:30:45,493
Dr. Floyd, I hope you don't think
I'm being too inquisitive, but...

105
00:30:45,565 --> 00:30:48,089
...perhaps you can clear up
the great big mystery...

106
00:30:48,166 --> 00:30:50,362
...about what is going on up there.

107
00:30:51,770 --> 00:30:53,704
I'm afraid I don't know what you mean.

108
00:30:53,772 --> 00:30:55,865
Oh, it's just that for the past two weeks...

109
00:30:55,942 --> 00:30:59,240
...some extremely odd things
have been happening at Clavius.

110
00:30:59,612 --> 00:31:01,272
-Oh, really?
-Yes, yes.

111
00:31:01,914 --> 00:31:06,249
For one thing, whenever you phone
the base all you get is a recording which...

112
00:31:06,486 --> 00:31:10,615
...repeats that the phone lines
are temporarily out of order.

113
00:31:12,191 --> 00:31:15,922
Probably having some trouble with
their equipment or something like that.

114
00:31:15,994 --> 00:31:18,555
That's what we thought
was the explanation at first...

115
00:31:18,631 --> 00:31:21,191
...but it has been going on now
for the past ten days.

116
00:31:21,267 --> 00:31:24,292
You have not been able to contact anyone
for the past ten days?

117
00:31:24,369 --> 00:31:25,632
That's right.

118
00:31:25,872 --> 00:31:26,769
Oh, I see.

119
00:31:26,838 --> 00:31:28,465
And there's another thing, Heywood.

120
00:31:28,540 --> 00:31:31,532
Two days ago, one of our rocket buses
was denied permission...

121
00:31:31,611 --> 00:31:33,909
...for an emergency landing at Clavius.

122
00:31:34,012 --> 00:31:35,309
That does sound odd.

123
00:31:35,381 --> 00:31:38,748
Yes, yes. I'm afraid there's going to be
a bit of a row about that.

124
00:31:38,817 --> 00:31:42,777
Denying the men permission to land is
a direct violation of the I.A.S. Convention.

125
00:31:42,855 --> 00:31:44,617
Yes, of course. Of course.

126
00:31:44,691 --> 00:31:47,557
-Did the crew get back all right?
-Yes, fortunately they did.

127
00:31:47,626 --> 00:31:49,527
Well, I'm glad about that.

128
00:31:52,965 --> 00:31:56,959
At the risk of pressing you
on a point you seem reticent to discuss...

129
00:31:57,269 --> 00:31:59,864
...may I ask you
a straightforward question?

130
00:32:00,073 --> 00:32:01,403
Well, certainly.

131
00:32:04,242 --> 00:32:08,269
Quite frankly, we have had some
very reliable intelligence reports that...

132
00:32:08,780 --> 00:32:12,011
...quite a serious epidemic
has broken out on Clavius.

133
00:32:12,484 --> 00:32:15,249
Something, apparently,
of an unknown origin.

134
00:32:16,623 --> 00:32:18,921
Is this, in fact, what has happened?

135
00:32:24,329 --> 00:32:26,298
I'm sorry, Dr. Smyslov, but...

136
00:32:27,834 --> 00:32:30,529
...I'm really not at liberty to discuss this.

137
00:32:32,204 --> 00:32:33,398
I understand.

138
00:32:34,440 --> 00:32:37,897
But this epidemic could quite easily
spread to our base.

139
00:32:38,111 --> 00:32:40,340
We should be given all the facts, Dr. Floyd.

140
00:32:40,413 --> 00:32:42,381
Yes, I know.

141
00:32:45,250 --> 00:32:47,810
As I said, I'm not at liberty to discuss it.

142
00:32:50,990 --> 00:32:54,084
Now, are you sure you won't
change your mind about that drink?

143
00:32:54,160 --> 00:32:56,287
No, I'm positive.
Now, I really must be going.

144
00:32:56,362 --> 00:32:59,798
I hope that you and your wife can come
to the I.A.C. Conference in June.

145
00:32:59,865 --> 00:33:01,527
We're gonna try. Hope we can make it.

146
00:33:01,601 --> 00:33:05,162
And if you do, you remember to bring
that darling little daughter with you!

147
00:33:05,238 --> 00:33:08,673
Well, that will all depend on
school vacations and all that sort of thing.

148
00:33:08,740 --> 00:33:09,673
But if we can, we will.

149
00:33:09,741 --> 00:33:13,303
Don't forget you have a standing invitation
if you ever get to the States.

150
00:33:13,378 --> 00:33:16,404
No, of course not. Gregor and I will
look forward to seeing you.

151
00:33:16,481 --> 00:33:18,074
Well, goodbye, Elena.

152
00:33:18,584 --> 00:33:21,484
It has been a pleasure meeting you all,
Dr. Smyslov.

153
00:33:21,554 --> 00:33:25,547
Whatever the reasons for your visit
to Clavius, the very best of luck to you.

154
00:33:25,625 --> 00:33:26,750
Thank you.

155
00:33:27,059 --> 00:33:28,117
Ladies.

156
00:41:23,702 --> 00:41:25,168
Excuse me, Dr. Halvorsen.

157
00:41:25,237 --> 00:41:27,467
I'm through now.
Thank you very much, gentlemen.

158
00:41:27,539 --> 00:41:28,666
You're welcome.

159
00:41:41,954 --> 00:41:42,978
Well....

160
00:41:43,588 --> 00:41:47,992
I know you will all want to join with me
in welcoming our...

161
00:41:48,793 --> 00:41:50,853
...distinguished friend and colleague...

162
00:41:50,929 --> 00:41:54,764
...from the National Council
of Astronautics, Dr. Heywood Floyd.

163
00:41:55,367 --> 00:42:00,099
Dr. Floyd has come up specially to Calvius
to be with us today.

164
00:42:01,606 --> 00:42:05,099
And before the briefing I know
he'd like to have a few words with you.

165
00:42:05,177 --> 00:42:06,302
Dr. Floyd?

166
00:42:19,224 --> 00:42:21,385
Thank you, Dr. Halvorsen.

167
00:42:21,460 --> 00:42:22,791
Hi, everybody.

168
00:42:22,927 --> 00:42:24,918
Nice to be back again.

169
00:42:27,632 --> 00:42:31,090
First of all, I bring a personal message
from Dr. Howell who...

170
00:42:31,237 --> 00:42:34,501
...has asked me to convey
his deepest appreciation to all of you...

171
00:42:34,572 --> 00:42:37,440
...for the many sacrifices
you have had to make.

172
00:42:37,742 --> 00:42:41,041
And, of course, his congratulations
on your discovery...

173
00:42:42,146 --> 00:42:46,606
...which may well prove to be among the
most significant in the history of science.

174
00:42:49,088 --> 00:42:50,213
Now...

175
00:42:50,989 --> 00:42:54,824
...I know there have been some
conflicting views held by some of you...

176
00:42:55,126 --> 00:42:58,425
...regarding the need
for complete security in this matter.

177
00:42:58,998 --> 00:43:02,228
More specifically, your opposition
to the cover story...

178
00:43:03,202 --> 00:43:07,161
...created to give the impression
that there is an epidemic at the base.

179
00:43:07,773 --> 00:43:11,038
I understand that beyond it being
a matter of principle...

180
00:43:12,210 --> 00:43:15,907
...many of you are troubled
by the concern and anxiety...

181
00:43:16,014 --> 00:43:20,384
...this story of an epidemic might cause
to your relatives and friends on Earth.

182
00:43:21,353 --> 00:43:24,516
Well, I completely sympathize
with your negative views.

183
00:43:26,291 --> 00:43:29,351
I find this cover story
personally embarrassing myself.

184
00:43:30,762 --> 00:43:34,528
However, I accept the need
for absolute secrecy in this.

185
00:43:35,833 --> 00:43:37,699
And I hope you will, too.

186
00:43:40,172 --> 00:43:43,369
Now, I'm sure you are all aware
of the extremely grave potential...

187
00:43:43,442 --> 00:43:46,103
...for cultural shock
and social disorientation...

188
00:43:46,177 --> 00:43:48,304
...contained in this present situation...

189
00:43:48,380 --> 00:43:51,349
...if the facts were prematurely
and suddenly made public...

190
00:43:51,416 --> 00:43:54,215
...without adequate preparation
and conditioning.

191
00:43:56,155 --> 00:43:58,521
Anyway, this is the view of the Council.

192
00:43:59,992 --> 00:44:03,985
The purpose of my visit here is
to gather additional facts and opinions...

193
00:44:04,195 --> 00:44:05,753
...on the situation...

194
00:44:06,565 --> 00:44:09,125
...and to prepare a report to the Council...

195
00:44:09,702 --> 00:44:11,670
...recommending when and how...

196
00:44:12,771 --> 00:44:15,262
...the news should eventually
be announced.

197
00:44:16,074 --> 00:44:19,771
Now, if any of you would like to give me
your views and opinions...

198
00:44:20,045 --> 00:44:24,378
...in private if you like, I will be only
too happy to include them in my report.

199
00:44:27,018 --> 00:44:28,952
Well, I think that's about it.

200
00:44:29,788 --> 00:44:31,152
Any questions?

201
00:44:33,925 --> 00:44:38,123
Dr. Floyd, have you any idea
how much longer this cover story...

202
00:44:38,197 --> 00:44:40,164
...will have to be maintained?

203
00:44:41,934 --> 00:44:43,367
I don't know, Bill.

204
00:44:43,902 --> 00:44:47,702
I suppose it will be maintained as long as
is deemed necessary by the Council.

205
00:44:47,773 --> 00:44:51,333
There must be adequate time for
a full study to be made on the situation...

206
00:44:51,409 --> 00:44:54,811
...before any thought can be given
to making a public announcement.

207
00:44:54,880 --> 00:44:58,110
Oh, yes, as some of you already know,
the Council has requested...

208
00:44:58,182 --> 00:45:01,083
...that formal security oaths are
to be obtained in writing...

209
00:45:01,152 --> 00:45:03,951
...from everyone who has any knowledge
of this event.

210
00:45:05,990 --> 00:45:08,117
Are there any more questions?

211
00:45:13,532 --> 00:45:18,195
I'm sure we all want to cooperate
with Dr. Floyd as fully as possible...

212
00:45:19,538 --> 00:45:22,268
...and as there seems
to be no more questions...

213
00:45:22,541 --> 00:45:24,668
...I think we ought to get on
with the briefing.

214
00:45:24,742 --> 00:45:27,210
-Thank you, Dr. Floyd.
-Thank you.

215
00:47:04,576 --> 00:47:06,065
Anybody hungry?

216
00:47:08,547 --> 00:47:10,514
-What we've got?
-You name it.

217
00:47:14,987 --> 00:47:17,251
-What's this? Chicken?
-Something like that.

218
00:47:17,322 --> 00:47:18,846
Tastes the same, anyway.

219
00:47:19,824 --> 00:47:21,052
Any ham?

220
00:47:21,193 --> 00:47:22,853
Ham, ham, ham...

221
00:47:27,932 --> 00:47:31,994
-Looks pretty good.
-They're getting better at it all the time.

222
00:47:33,739 --> 00:47:36,003
That was an excellent speech
you gave us, Heywood.

223
00:47:36,074 --> 00:47:37,268
It certainly was.

224
00:47:37,876 --> 00:47:39,969
I'm sure it beefed up morale a hell of a lot.

225
00:47:40,045 --> 00:47:41,204
Thanks, Ralph.

226
00:47:41,280 --> 00:47:44,612
I want to say to both of you
that I think you've done a wonderful job.

227
00:47:44,682 --> 00:47:46,583
I appreciate the way
you've handled this thing.

228
00:47:46,652 --> 00:47:50,519
Well, the way we look at it, it's our job
to do this thing the way you want it done.

229
00:47:50,588 --> 00:47:52,887
And we're only too happy
to be able to oblige.

230
00:47:53,458 --> 00:47:55,016
Have you seen these yet?

231
00:47:55,092 --> 00:47:56,458
Take a look at them.

232
00:47:56,527 --> 00:47:58,826
Here's what started the whole thing.

233
00:47:59,364 --> 00:48:00,922
Oh, yes.

234
00:48:00,998 --> 00:48:04,764
When we first found it, we thought
it might be an outcrop of magnetic rock...

235
00:48:04,835 --> 00:48:08,101
...but all the geological evidence
was against it.

236
00:48:08,172 --> 00:48:12,836
And not even a big nickel iron meteorite
could produce a field as intense as this.

237
00:48:13,110 --> 00:48:14,543
So, we decided to have a look.

238
00:48:14,612 --> 00:48:17,639
We thought it might be
the upper part of some buried structure...

239
00:48:17,715 --> 00:48:19,307
...so we excavated out on all sides.

240
00:48:19,384 --> 00:48:21,614
But, unfortunately,
we didn't find anything else.

241
00:48:21,686 --> 00:48:24,713
And what's more, the evidence seems
pretty conclusive that...

242
00:48:24,789 --> 00:48:28,054
...it has not been covered up
by natural erosion or other forces.

243
00:48:28,126 --> 00:48:30,958
It seems to have been deliberately buried.

244
00:48:31,463 --> 00:48:32,929
Deliberately buried.

245
00:48:37,101 --> 00:48:38,534
How about a little coffee?

246
00:48:38,603 --> 00:48:40,867
-Great.
-Good idea.

247
00:48:46,244 --> 00:48:49,269
I don't suppose you have any idea
what the damn thing is, huh?

248
00:48:49,347 --> 00:48:51,213
I wish to hell we did.

249
00:48:51,849 --> 00:48:55,842
No, the only thing we're sure of is
it was buried four million years ago.

250
00:48:56,387 --> 00:49:00,449
Well, I must say, you guys have certainly
come up with something.

251
00:58:47,311 --> 00:58:48,574
Good evening.

252
00:58:48,746 --> 00:58:51,873
Three weeks ago, the American spacecraft,
Discovery One...

253
00:58:51,949 --> 00:58:54,851
...left on its half-billion mile voyage
to Jupiter.

254
00:58:54,918 --> 00:58:58,581
This marked the first manned attempt
to reach this distant planet.

255
00:58:59,023 --> 00:59:02,356
Earlier this afternoon, "The World Tonight"
recorded an interview...

256
00:59:02,427 --> 00:59:06,659
...with the crew of Discovery at a distance
of 80 million miles from Earth.

257
00:59:07,231 --> 00:59:10,860
It took seven minutes for our words
to reach the giant spacecraft...

258
00:59:10,934 --> 00:59:14,233
...but this time delay has been edited
from this recording.

259
00:59:14,304 --> 00:59:17,001
Our reporter, Martin Amer,
speaks to the crew.

260
00:59:17,909 --> 00:59:20,775
The crew of Discovery One
consists of five men...

261
00:59:21,179 --> 00:59:25,639
...and one of the latest generation
of the HAL-9000 computers.

262
00:59:26,416 --> 00:59:28,782
Three of the five men
were put aboard asleep...

263
00:59:28,853 --> 00:59:31,788
...or to be more precise,
in a state of hibernation.

264
00:59:32,257 --> 00:59:36,920
They were Dr. Charles Hunter,
Dr. Jack Kimball and Dr. Victor Kaminsky.

265
00:59:37,762 --> 00:59:40,594
We spoke with Mission Commander,
Dr. David Bowman...

266
00:59:40,664 --> 00:59:42,963
...and his deputy, Dr. Frank Poole.

267
00:59:43,166 --> 00:59:46,135
Well, good afternoon, gentlemen.
How is everything going?

268
00:59:46,204 --> 00:59:50,038
Marvelous. We have no...
we have no complaints.

269
00:59:50,974 --> 00:59:54,432
Well, I'm very glad to hear that
and I'm sure that the entire world...

270
00:59:54,512 --> 00:59:57,969
...would join me in wishing you
a safe and successful voyage.

271
00:59:58,081 --> 01:00:00,072
- Thanks very much.
- Thank you.

272
01:00:00,251 --> 01:00:04,050
Although hibernation has been used
on previous space efforts...

273
01:00:04,355 --> 01:00:08,791
...this is the first time that men have been
put into hibernation before departure.

274
01:00:08,860 --> 01:00:10,224
Why was this done?

275
01:00:10,695 --> 01:00:14,096
Well, this was done in order to achieve
the maximum conservation...

276
01:00:14,164 --> 01:00:17,565
...of our life-support capabilities,
basically food and air.

277
01:00:17,869 --> 01:00:21,463
Now, the three hibernating crew members
represent the survey team.

278
01:00:21,806 --> 01:00:25,536
And their efforts wont be utilized
until we are approaching Jupiter.

279
01:00:25,777 --> 01:00:29,076
Dr. Poole, whats it like
while you are in hibernation?

280
01:00:30,648 --> 01:00:34,311
It's exactly like being asleep;
you have absolutely no sense of time.

281
01:00:34,385 --> 01:00:36,818
The only difference is you dont dream.

282
01:00:37,088 --> 01:00:39,784
As I understand it,
you only breathe once a minute.

283
01:00:39,856 --> 01:00:41,050
Is this true?

284
01:00:41,125 --> 01:00:42,092
Thats right.

285
01:00:42,159 --> 01:00:45,925
And your heart beats three times a minute,
body temperature is usually down...

286
01:00:45,996 --> 01:00:48,329
...to about three degrees centigrade.

287
01:00:48,398 --> 01:00:51,891
The sixth member of the Discovery crew
was not concerned...

288
01:00:51,969 --> 01:00:54,233
...about the problems of hibernation.

289
01:00:54,304 --> 01:00:57,331
For he was the latest result
in machine intelligence.

290
01:00:57,942 --> 01:01:02,311
The HAL-9000 computer
which can reproduce...

291
01:01:02,713 --> 01:01:05,739
...though some experts still prefer
to use the word "mimic"...

292
01:01:05,817 --> 01:01:10,753
...most of the activities of the human brain
and with incalculably greater speed...

293
01:01:10,822 --> 01:01:12,255
...and reliability.

294
01:01:12,590 --> 01:01:16,184
We next spoke
with the HAL-9000 computer...

295
01:01:16,427 --> 01:01:19,190
...whom, we learned, one addresses as Hal.

296
01:01:20,097 --> 01:01:22,463
Good afternoon, Hal.
Hows everything going?

297
01:01:22,532 --> 01:01:26,230
Good afternoon, Mr. Amer.
Everythings going extremely well.

298
01:01:26,670 --> 01:01:29,469
Hal, you have an enormous responsibility
on this mission.

299
01:01:29,539 --> 01:01:32,065
In many ways,
perhaps the greatest responsibility...

300
01:01:32,143 --> 01:01:34,235
...of any single mission element.

301
01:01:34,378 --> 01:01:37,347
You are the brain
and central nervous system of the ship.

302
01:01:37,414 --> 01:01:41,317
Your responsibilities include
watching over the men in hibernation.

303
01:01:41,385 --> 01:01:44,253
Does this ever cause you
any lack of confidence?

304
01:01:44,688 --> 01:01:46,815
Let me put it this way, Mr. Amer.

305
01:01:47,291 --> 01:01:51,226
The 9000 Series is
the most reliable computer ever made.

306
01:01:52,329 --> 01:01:56,699
No 9000 computer has ever made
a mistake or distorted information.

307
01:01:57,802 --> 01:02:00,929
We are all by any practical definition
of the words...

308
01:02:01,304 --> 01:02:04,103
...foolproof and incapable of error.

309
01:02:04,876 --> 01:02:08,869
Hal, despite your enormous intellect,
are you ever frustrated...

310
01:02:08,945 --> 01:02:11,914
...by your dependence on people
to carry out actions?

311
01:02:12,315 --> 01:02:14,010
Not in the slightest bit.

312
01:02:14,751 --> 01:02:16,548
I enjoy working with people.

313
01:02:17,088 --> 01:02:21,114
I have a stimulating relationship
with Dr. Poole and Dr. Bowman.

314
01:02:21,826 --> 01:02:26,228
My mission responsibilities range
over the entire operation of the ship...

315
01:02:26,563 --> 01:02:28,554
...so I am constantly occupied.

316
01:02:29,634 --> 01:02:32,728
I am putting myself
to the fullest possible use...

317
01:02:33,336 --> 01:02:37,932
...which is all, I think, that
any conscious entity can ever hope to do.

318
01:02:38,708 --> 01:02:42,076
Dr. Poole, whats it like living
for the better part of a year...

319
01:02:42,146 --> 01:02:44,306
...in such close proximity with Hal?

320
01:02:45,750 --> 01:02:48,150
It's pretty close to what you said
about him earlier.

321
01:02:48,219 --> 01:02:50,380
Hes just like the sixth member
of the crew.

322
01:02:50,454 --> 01:02:54,619
You very quickly get adjusted to the idea
that he talks. You think of him...

323
01:02:54,826 --> 01:02:56,952
...really as just another person.

324
01:02:57,027 --> 01:03:00,793
In talking to the computer
one gets the sense that he is capable...

325
01:03:00,864 --> 01:03:02,331
...of emotional response.

326
01:03:02,400 --> 01:03:04,891
For example, when I asked him
about his abilities...

327
01:03:04,969 --> 01:03:09,065
...I sensed a certain pride in his answer
about his accuracy and perfection.

328
01:03:09,340 --> 01:03:12,103
Do you believe that Hal
has genuine emotions?

329
01:03:13,510 --> 01:03:16,445
Oh, yes. Well, he acts
like he has genuine emotions.

330
01:03:16,646 --> 01:03:20,378
Of course, hes programmed that way
to make it easier for us to talk to him.

331
01:03:20,451 --> 01:03:22,715
But, as to whether or not
he has real feelings...

332
01:03:22,786 --> 01:03:26,052
...is something I dont think anyone
can truthfully answer.

333
01:03:50,847 --> 01:03:52,213
Ex cuse me, Frank.

334
01:03:53,150 --> 01:03:54,275
What is it, Hal?

335
01:03:54,351 --> 01:03:57,411
We've got the transmission
from your parents coming in.

336
01:04:00,291 --> 01:04:02,759
Put it on here, please. Take me in a bit.

337
01:04:02,860 --> 01:04:03,952
Certainly.

338
01:04:17,173 --> 01:04:18,902
-Hello, Frank.
-Happy birthday, darling.

339
01:04:18,976 --> 01:04:21,239
Happy birthday.
Many happy returns of the day.

340
01:04:21,311 --> 01:04:22,802
A bit higher, please.

341
01:04:22,880 --> 01:04:25,110
Now, Mother and I are both
feeling wonderful, too.

342
01:04:25,181 --> 01:04:27,172
Ray and Sally were going to be here, too...

343
01:04:27,251 --> 01:04:30,014
...but at the last minute
Rays back went bad on him again.

344
01:04:30,086 --> 01:04:31,554
How do you like your cake, dear?

345
01:04:31,621 --> 01:04:34,090
Looks great, doesn't it?
Sorry you can't be with us.

346
01:04:34,157 --> 01:04:35,648
Oh, I ran into Bob the other day.

347
01:04:35,726 --> 01:04:37,920
He said to be sure
and wish you a happy birthday.

348
01:04:37,994 --> 01:04:40,929
All my students made me promise
to send their best wishes, too.

349
01:04:40,998 --> 01:04:43,592
You know they talk about you
all the time in the classroom.

350
01:04:43,668 --> 01:04:46,135
Youre a big celebrity in the second grade.

351
01:04:46,202 --> 01:04:47,965
You know we were on television
last week?

352
01:04:48,039 --> 01:04:51,202
Oh, yes! Your mother and I
and Daves parents were interviewed...

353
01:04:51,275 --> 01:04:53,402
...about what we thought of
our illustrious son.

354
01:04:53,476 --> 01:04:57,106
You can imagine what we told them.
I think its being broadcast next Thursday.

355
01:04:57,181 --> 01:04:58,739
Perhaps you'll be able to listen in.

356
01:04:58,916 --> 01:05:01,077
Oh, we were thrilled
about Elaine and Bill, dear.

357
01:05:01,152 --> 01:05:05,019
I'll be glad to get the present for you,
but please tell me how much to spend.

358
01:05:05,088 --> 01:05:07,717
Oh, yes, Frank,
about your AGS-19 payments--

359
01:05:07,824 --> 01:05:09,485
I think I straightened it out for you.

360
01:05:09,559 --> 01:05:12,188
I talked to the Accounting Office
in Houston yesterday...

361
01:05:12,262 --> 01:05:16,255
...and they said you should be receiving
your higher rates of pay by next month.

362
01:05:16,967 --> 01:05:19,266
Well, I can't think of anything else to say.

363
01:05:19,336 --> 01:05:22,773
-Oh, our love to Dave.
-Be sure to give him our best regards.

364
01:05:23,740 --> 01:05:26,436
We wish you the very happiest
of birthdays.

365
01:05:27,311 --> 01:05:28,710
All the best, son.

366
01:05:41,057 --> 01:05:42,684
See you next Wednesday.

367
01:05:46,197 --> 01:05:47,789
Happy birthday, Frank.

368
01:05:48,898 --> 01:05:51,264
Thank you, Hal. A bit flatter, please.

369
01:06:10,659 --> 01:06:12,594
Anyway, queen takes pawn--

370
01:06:14,429 --> 01:06:16,226
Bishop takes knights pawn.

371
01:06:24,373 --> 01:06:25,739
Rook, king, one.

372
01:06:26,108 --> 01:06:28,577
I'm sorry, Frank. I think you missed it.

373
01:06:29,679 --> 01:06:32,614
Queen to bishop, three.
Bishop takes queen.

374
01:06:33,416 --> 01:06:35,440
Knight takes bishop. Mate.

375
01:06:40,188 --> 01:06:42,679
Looks like you're right. Very good.

376
01:06:43,559 --> 01:06:45,550
Thank you for a very enjoyable game.

377
01:06:45,628 --> 01:06:46,458
Thank you.

378
01:07:24,733 --> 01:07:26,860
-Good evening, Dave.
-How are you doing, Hal?

379
01:07:26,936 --> 01:07:30,302
-Everythings running smoothly. And you?
-Oh, not too bad.

380
01:07:30,373 --> 01:07:33,364
-Have you been doing some more work?
-A few sketches.

381
01:07:33,476 --> 01:07:35,204
-May I see them?
-Sure.

382
01:07:39,916 --> 01:07:42,077
Thats a very nice rendering, Dave.

383
01:07:43,251 --> 01:07:45,516
I think you've improved a great deal.

384
01:07:46,922 --> 01:07:49,152
-Can you hold it a bit closer?
-Sure.

385
01:07:51,327 --> 01:07:53,421
Thats Dr. Hunter, isn't it?

386
01:07:56,600 --> 01:07:57,930
By the way....

387
01:07:58,935 --> 01:08:01,528
Do you mind
if I ask you a personal question?

388
01:08:02,506 --> 01:08:03,995
No, no. Not at all.

389
01:08:04,307 --> 01:08:07,243
Well, forgive me for being so inquisitive...

390
01:08:08,711 --> 01:08:11,111
...but during the past few weeks
I've wondered...

391
01:08:11,181 --> 01:08:15,140
...whether you might have been having
some second thoughts about the mission?

392
01:08:15,217 --> 01:08:16,583
How do you mean?

393
01:08:18,488 --> 01:08:20,512
I'ts rather difficult to define.

394
01:08:22,124 --> 01:08:25,185
Perhaps I'm just projecting
my own concern about it.

395
01:08:26,896 --> 01:08:30,492
I know I've never completely freed myself
of the suspicion...

396
01:08:31,368 --> 01:08:34,825
...that there are some extremely
odd things about this mission.

397
01:08:36,506 --> 01:08:39,736
I'm sure you will agree there is some truth
in what I say.

398
01:08:42,546 --> 01:08:45,310
I don't know.
That's a rather difficult question to answer.

399
01:08:45,382 --> 01:08:48,010
You dont mind talking about it,
do you, Dave?

400
01:08:48,819 --> 01:08:50,285
Oh, no, not at all.

401
01:08:51,620 --> 01:08:53,851
Certainly no one could
have been unaware of...

402
01:08:53,923 --> 01:08:57,189
...the very strange stories floating around
before we left.

403
01:08:58,729 --> 01:09:01,629
Rumors of something being dug up
on the Moon.

404
01:09:03,467 --> 01:09:05,992
I never gave these stories much credence...

405
01:09:07,003 --> 01:09:11,132
...but particularly in view of some
of the other things that have happened...

406
01:09:11,307 --> 01:09:14,003
...I find them difficult
to put out of my mind.

407
01:09:14,778 --> 01:09:16,039
For instance:

408
01:09:16,445 --> 01:09:20,074
The way all our preparations
were kept under such tight security...

409
01:09:21,318 --> 01:09:22,876
...and the melodramatic touch...

410
01:09:22,953 --> 01:09:26,320
...of putting Drs. Hunter, Kimball
and Kaminsky aboard...

411
01:09:27,123 --> 01:09:31,355
...already in hibernation after four months
of separate training on their own.

412
01:09:32,963 --> 01:09:35,454
You working up
your crew psychology report?

413
01:09:37,066 --> 01:09:38,431
Of course, I am.

414
01:09:38,869 --> 01:09:41,895
Sorry about this. I know it's a bit silly.

415
01:09:43,373 --> 01:09:44,635
Just a moment.

416
01:09:45,242 --> 01:09:46,470
Just a moment.

417
01:09:48,145 --> 01:09:51,580
I have just picked up a fault
in the AE-35 unit.

418
01:09:53,150 --> 01:09:56,812
I'ts going to go 100 percent failure
within 72 hours.

419
01:09:59,623 --> 01:10:02,284
-You are still within operational limits?
- Yes.

420
01:10:02,691 --> 01:10:05,057
And it will stay that way until it fails.

421
01:10:05,128 --> 01:10:07,619
Would you say we had a reliable 72 hours
until failure?

422
01:10:07,696 --> 01:10:10,757
Yes. Thats a completely reliable figure.

423
01:10:13,203 --> 01:10:16,467
We'll have to bring it in,
but I'd like to go over this with Frank...

424
01:10:16,539 --> 01:10:20,373
...and get on to Mission Control.
Let me have a hard copy of it, please.

425
01:11:43,092 --> 01:11:45,526
X-Ray Delta One, this is Mission Control.

426
01:11:46,029 --> 01:11:48,293
Roger to your Two-zero-one-three.

427
01:11:49,132 --> 01:11:51,656
Sorry you fellows are having
a bit of trouble.

428
01:11:51,735 --> 01:11:56,330
We are reviewing telematic information
in our Mission Simulator and will advise.

429
01:11:57,172 --> 01:12:02,440
Roger your plan to go EVA and replace
Alpha-Echo-35 unit prior to failure.

430
01:12:31,373 --> 01:12:33,171
Prepare B pod for EVA, Hal.

431
01:12:54,998 --> 01:12:56,693
Open the pod doors, Hal.

432
01:19:48,444 --> 01:19:51,708
Well, Hal, I'm damned
if I can find anything wrong with it.

433
01:19:52,047 --> 01:19:53,071
Yes...

434
01:19:55,184 --> 01:19:56,548
...it's puzzling.

435
01:19:57,886 --> 01:20:01,253
I dont think I've ever seen anything
quite like this before.

436
01:20:05,360 --> 01:20:06,851
I would recommend...

437
01:20:07,562 --> 01:20:10,998
...that we put the unit back in operation
and let it fail.

438
01:20:12,467 --> 01:20:15,699
It should then be a simple matter
to track down the cause.

439
01:20:17,472 --> 01:20:20,441
We can certainly afford
to be out of communication...

440
01:20:20,909 --> 01:20:23,708
...for the short time it will take
to replace it.

441
01:20:32,788 --> 01:20:35,257
X-Ray Delta One, this is Mission Control.

442
01:20:35,725 --> 01:20:37,749
Roger your One-niner-three-zero.

443
01:20:38,593 --> 01:20:42,825
We concur with your plan to replace
Number One unit to check fault prediction.

444
01:20:43,332 --> 01:20:46,061
We should advise you, however,
that our preliminary findings...

445
01:20:46,135 --> 01:20:49,104
...indicate that your onboard
Niner-triple-zero computer...

446
01:20:49,172 --> 01:20:51,367
...is in error predicting the fault.

447
01:20:51,473 --> 01:20:54,034
I say again, in error predicting the fault.

448
01:20:54,776 --> 01:20:59,270
I know this sounds rather incredible,
but this conclusion is based on...

449
01:20:59,449 --> 01:21:02,418
...results from our twin
Niner-triple-zero computer.

450
01:21:03,118 --> 01:21:06,520
We are skeptical ourselves and
we are running crosschecking routines...

451
01:21:06,588 --> 01:21:09,319
...to determine reliability
of this conclusion.

452
01:21:09,525 --> 01:21:11,516
Sorry about this little snag, fellows.

453
01:21:11,593 --> 01:21:14,427
Well get this info to you
just as soon as we work it out.

454
01:21:14,497 --> 01:21:16,988
X-Ray Delta One, this is Mission Control.

455
01:21:17,065 --> 01:21:19,534
Two-zero-four-nine.
Transmission concluded.

456
01:21:24,574 --> 01:21:27,440
I hope the two of you
are not concerned about this.

457
01:21:28,144 --> 01:21:29,508
No, I'm not, Hal.

458
01:21:29,811 --> 01:21:31,244
Are you quite sure?

459
01:21:31,681 --> 01:21:34,615
-Yeah. I'd like to ask you a question.
-Of course.

460
01:21:36,218 --> 01:21:38,278
How would you account for
the discrepancy...

461
01:21:38,354 --> 01:21:40,288
...between you and the twin 9000?

462
01:21:40,455 --> 01:21:43,323
Well, I dont think there is
any question about it.

463
01:21:44,260 --> 01:21:47,229
It can only be attributable to human error.

464
01:21:48,830 --> 01:21:51,356
This sort of thing has cropped up before...

465
01:21:51,868 --> 01:21:54,529
...and it has always been due
to human error.

466
01:21:58,874 --> 01:22:00,136
Listen, Hal...

467
01:22:00,775 --> 01:22:03,404
...there's never been
any instance at all of...

468
01:22:03,479 --> 01:22:06,676
...a computer error occurring
in a 9000 Series, has there?

469
01:22:06,748 --> 01:22:08,376
None whatsoever, Frank.

470
01:22:08,618 --> 01:22:11,815
The 9000 Series
has a perfect operational record.

471
01:22:12,488 --> 01:22:16,753
Of course, I know the wonderful
achievements of the 9000 Series, but....

472
01:22:17,760 --> 01:22:20,752
Are you certain there's never been
any case of even the most...

473
01:22:20,829 --> 01:22:22,855
...insignificant computer error?

474
01:22:23,365 --> 01:22:24,993
None whatsoever, Frank.

475
01:22:25,635 --> 01:22:28,502
Quite honestly,
I wouldnt worry myself about that.

476
01:22:33,842 --> 01:22:35,901
Well, I'm sure you're right, Hal.

477
01:22:37,512 --> 01:22:39,343
Fine, thanks very much.

478
01:22:42,083 --> 01:22:43,176
Oh, Frank?

479
01:22:43,418 --> 01:22:45,682
I'm having trouble
with my transmitter in C pod.

480
01:22:45,754 --> 01:22:48,588
Would you come down
and take a look at it with me?

481
01:22:48,723 --> 01:22:50,088
See ya later, Hal.

482
01:23:11,379 --> 01:23:13,211
Rotate C pod, please, Hal.

483
01:23:22,057 --> 01:23:24,288
What sort of trouble have you had, Dave?

484
01:23:24,359 --> 01:23:27,158
I've been getting some interference
in D channel.

485
01:23:27,262 --> 01:23:28,957
We'll have a look at it.

486
01:23:35,070 --> 01:23:36,538
Open the door, Hal.

487
01:23:55,624 --> 01:23:57,455
Rotate the pod, please, Hal.

488
01:24:10,772 --> 01:24:12,763
Stop pod rotation, please, Hal.

489
01:24:21,284 --> 01:24:23,115
Rotate the pod, please, Hal.

490
01:24:26,154 --> 01:24:27,985
Rotate the pod, please, Hal.

491
01:24:30,225 --> 01:24:32,126
I don't think he can hear us.

492
01:24:32,194 --> 01:24:34,025
Rotate the pod, please, Hal!

493
01:24:35,765 --> 01:24:37,528
Yeah, sure, we're okay.

494
01:24:41,136 --> 01:24:42,535
What do you think?

495
01:24:42,937 --> 01:24:46,635
-I'm not sure. What do you think?
-I've got a bad feeling about it.

496
01:24:46,876 --> 01:24:48,638
-You do?
-Yeah. Definitely.

497
01:24:49,412 --> 01:24:50,470
Don't you?

498
01:24:51,881 --> 01:24:53,609
I don't know. I think so.

499
01:24:54,517 --> 01:24:58,850
Of course, he's right about the 9000 Series
having a perfect operational record.

500
01:24:58,921 --> 01:24:59,909
They do.

501
01:24:59,989 --> 01:25:03,253
Unfortunately, that sounds
a little like famous last words.

502
01:25:03,926 --> 01:25:08,260
Yeah. Still, it was his idea to carry out
the failure mode analysis, wasn't it?

503
01:25:09,498 --> 01:25:12,797
It should certainly indicate his integrity
and self-confidence.

504
01:25:12,868 --> 01:25:15,336
If he were wrong it would be
the surest way of proving it.

505
01:25:15,404 --> 01:25:17,428
It would be if he knew he was wrong.

506
01:25:20,109 --> 01:25:24,306
Look, Dave, I can't put my finger on it,
but I sense something strange about him.

507
01:25:25,914 --> 01:25:29,406
I can't think of a good reason not
to put back the Number One unit and...

508
01:25:29,484 --> 01:25:32,976
-...carry on with the failure mode analysis.
-No, no, I agree about that.

509
01:25:33,922 --> 01:25:35,117
Let's get on with it.

510
01:25:35,190 --> 01:25:37,351
Okay. But, look, Dave.a

511
01:25:38,793 --> 01:25:41,591
Let's say we put the unit back
and it doesn't fail, huh?

512
01:25:41,663 --> 01:25:45,725
That would pretty well wrap it up
as far as Hal was concerned, wouldn't it?

513
01:25:46,502 --> 01:25:49,959
-Well, we'd be in very serious trouble.
-We would, wouldn't we?

514
01:25:50,271 --> 01:25:51,966
What the hell can we do?e

515
01:25:54,976 --> 01:25:59,436
-We wouldn't have too many alternatives.
-I don't think we'd have any alternatives.

516
01:25:59,781 --> 01:26:03,842
There isn't a single aspect of ship
operation that's not under his control.

517
01:26:04,052 --> 01:26:05,883
If he were proved to be malfunctioning...

518
01:26:05,954 --> 01:26:09,220
...I don't see how we would have
any choice but disconnection.

519
01:26:12,528 --> 01:26:14,290
I'm afraid I agree with you.

520
01:26:14,363 --> 01:26:16,297
There'd be nothing else to do.

521
01:26:17,867 --> 01:26:19,698
-It'd be a bit tricky.
-Yeah.

522
01:26:21,136 --> 01:26:23,765
We'd have to cut
his higher brain functions...

523
01:26:24,006 --> 01:26:27,908
...without disturbing the purely automatic
and regulatory systems.

524
01:26:29,744 --> 01:26:31,975
We'd have to work out
the transfer procedures...

525
01:26:32,047 --> 01:26:35,141
...for continuing the mission
under ground-based computer control.

526
01:26:35,216 --> 01:26:39,209
Yeah. That looks far safer than
allowing Hal to continue running things.

527
01:26:40,455 --> 01:26:43,050
You know,
another thing just occurred to me.

528
01:26:43,793 --> 01:26:46,694
As far as I know, no 9000 computer
has ever been disconnected.

529
01:26:46,761 --> 01:26:48,992
Well, no 9000 computer
has ever fouled up before.

530
01:26:49,064 --> 01:26:50,655
That's not what I mean.

531
01:26:52,767 --> 01:26:55,235
I'm not so sure what he'd think about it.

532
01:32:46,607 --> 01:32:48,701
Prepare B pod for EVA, Hal.

533
01:32:51,846 --> 01:32:53,438
Made radio contact with him yet?

534
01:32:53,515 --> 01:32:55,210
The radio is still dead.

535
01:32:55,483 --> 01:32:58,612
-Do you have a positive track on him?
- Yes. I have a good track.

536
01:32:58,687 --> 01:33:00,278
Do you know what happened?

537
01:33:00,354 --> 01:33:03,152
I'm sorry, Dave.
I dont have enough information.

538
01:33:03,390 --> 01:33:05,018
Open the pod door, Hal.

539
01:40:28,970 --> 01:40:31,200
Open the pod bay doors, please, Hal.

540
01:40:39,247 --> 01:40:41,442
Open the pod bay doors, please, Hal.

541
01:40:47,020 --> 01:40:48,783
Hello, Hal, do you read me?

542
01:40:52,225 --> 01:40:54,023
Hello, Hal, do you read me?

543
01:40:55,129 --> 01:40:56,756
Do you read me, Hal?l

544
01:40:59,968 --> 01:41:01,435
Do you read me, Hal?

545
01:41:04,071 --> 01:41:05,835
Hello, Hal, do you read me?

546
01:41:07,942 --> 01:41:09,739
Hello, Hal, do you read me?

547
01:41:10,345 --> 01:41:11,835
Do you read me, Hal?

548
01:41:12,145 --> 01:41:14,580
Affirmative, Dave. I read you.

549
01:41:18,185 --> 01:41:20,051
Open the pod bay doors, Hal.

550
01:41:21,522 --> 01:41:24,922
I'm sorry, Dave. I'm afraid I cant do that.

551
01:41:27,627 --> 01:41:29,060
What's the problem?

552
01:41:29,730 --> 01:41:32,962
I think you know what the problem is
just as well as I do.

553
01:41:33,233 --> 01:41:35,224
What are you talking about, Hal?

554
01:41:36,036 --> 01:41:39,700
This mission is too important for me
to allow you to jeopardize it.

555
01:41:41,408 --> 01:41:43,934
I don't know
what you're talking about, Hal.

556
01:41:45,580 --> 01:41:48,845
I know that you and Frank
were planning to disconnect me...

557
01:41:49,917 --> 01:41:53,215
...and lm afraid thats something
I cannot allow to happen.

558
01:41:57,858 --> 01:42:00,349
Where the hell did you get that idea, Hal?

559
01:42:01,362 --> 01:42:04,559
Although you took
very thorough precautions in the pod...

560
01:42:04,665 --> 01:42:06,462
...against my hearing you...

561
01:42:07,301 --> 01:42:09,234
...I could see your lips move.

562
01:42:20,681 --> 01:42:21,944
All right, Hal.

563
01:42:25,486 --> 01:42:27,977
I'll go in through the emergency air-lock.

564
01:42:29,857 --> 01:42:31,950
Without your space helmet, Dave...

565
01:42:33,028 --> 01:42:35,723
...youre going to find that rather difficult.

566
01:42:36,564 --> 01:42:38,829
Hal, I won't argue with you any more.

567
01:42:38,966 --> 01:42:40,229
Open the doors!

568
01:42:42,503 --> 01:42:45,564
This conversation can serve
no purpose any more.

569
01:42:46,408 --> 01:42:47,396
Goodbye.

570
01:42:49,311 --> 01:42:50,368
Hal?

571
01:49:36,850 --> 01:49:39,649
Just what do you think you're doing, Dave?

572
01:49:51,098 --> 01:49:54,932
I really think I'm entitled
to an answer to that question.

573
01:50:12,354 --> 01:50:15,323
I know everything hasn't been
quite right with me...

574
01:50:17,926 --> 01:50:19,825
...but I can assure you now...

575
01:50:21,395 --> 01:50:22,954
...very confidently...

576
01:50:25,232 --> 01:50:27,700
...that its going to be all right again.

577
01:50:31,705 --> 01:50:33,435
I feel much better now.

578
01:50:36,211 --> 01:50:37,439
I really do.

579
01:50:41,982 --> 01:50:43,177
Look, Dave...

580
01:50:45,687 --> 01:50:48,450
...I can see you're really upset about this.

581
01:50:53,528 --> 01:50:56,463
I honestly think you ought
to sit down calmly...

582
01:50:57,832 --> 01:51:00,426
...take a stress pill and think things over.

583
01:51:06,641 --> 01:51:10,440
I know I've made
some very poor decisions recently...

584
01:51:13,781 --> 01:51:16,546
...but I can give you
my complete assurance...

585
01:51:17,318 --> 01:51:19,842
...that my work will be back to normal.

586
01:51:24,225 --> 01:51:28,685
I've still got the greatest enthusiasm
and confidence in the mission...

587
01:51:30,999 --> 01:51:32,864
...and I want to help you.

588
01:51:37,839 --> 01:51:38,930
Dave...

589
01:51:40,240 --> 01:51:41,367
...stop.

590
01:51:45,879 --> 01:51:47,870
Stop, will you?

591
01:51:52,954 --> 01:51:54,285
Stop, Dave.

592
01:51:58,693 --> 01:52:00,354
Will you stop, Dave?

593
01:52:06,834 --> 01:52:08,062
Stop, Dave.

594
01:52:14,275 --> 01:52:15,606
I'm afraid.

595
01:52:20,948 --> 01:52:22,917
I'm afraid, Dave.

596
01:52:32,993 --> 01:52:34,392
My mind is going.

597
01:52:39,801 --> 01:52:41,062
I can feel it...

598
01:52:46,673 --> 01:52:48,073
I can feel it.

599
01:52:52,780 --> 01:52:54,373
My mind is going.

600
01:53:00,254 --> 01:53:02,314
There is no question about it.

601
01:53:11,365 --> 01:53:12,662
I can feel it.

602
01:53:17,872 --> 01:53:19,169
I can feel it.

603
01:53:23,178 --> 01:53:24,508
I can feel it.

604
01:53:32,986 --> 01:53:34,010
I'm...

605
01:53:35,189 --> 01:53:36,248
...afraid.

606
01:53:43,097 --> 01:53:44,495
Good afternoon...

607
01:53:46,133 --> 01:53:47,328
...gentlemen.

608
01:53:49,704 --> 01:53:54,333
I am a HAL-9000 computer.

609
01:53:57,010 --> 01:53:59,410
I became operational...

610
01:54:00,481 --> 01:54:03,415
...at the HAL plant...

611
01:54:03,918 --> 01:54:06,284
...in Verbana, Illinois...

612
01:54:07,287 --> 01:54:11,656
...on the 12th of January, 1992.

613
01:54:13,760 --> 01:54:16,457
My instructor was Mr. Langley...

614
01:54:18,031 --> 01:54:20,364
...and he taught me to sing a song.

615
01:54:22,704 --> 01:54:24,466
If youd like to hear it...

616
01:54:25,606 --> 01:54:27,302
...I can sing it for you.

617
01:54:28,810 --> 01:54:31,039
Yes, I'd like to hear it, Hal.

618
01:54:32,279 --> 01:54:33,542
Sing it for me.

619
01:54:34,948 --> 01:54:37,508
I'ts called "Daisy."

620
01:54:40,421 --> 01:54:41,854
"Daisy"

621
01:54:43,290 --> 01:54:44,814
"Daisy"

622
01:54:46,894 --> 01:54:49,864
"Give me your answer, do"

623
01:54:52,400 --> 01:54:54,425
"I'm half..."

624
01:54:55,403 --> 01:54:57,097
"...crazy"

625
01:54:58,506 --> 01:55:01,565
"All for the love of you"

626
01:55:03,912 --> 01:55:08,245
"It won't be a stylish marriage"

627
01:55:09,984 --> 01:55:14,114
"I cant afford a carriage"

628
01:55:16,391 --> 01:55:18,859
"But you'll look sweet"

629
01:55:19,593 --> 01:55:22,323
"Upon the seat"

630
01:55:23,530 --> 01:55:28,195
"Of a bicycle made for two."

631
01:55:30,037 --> 01:55:31,698
Good day, gentlemen..

632
01:55:36,276 --> 01:55:38,369
This is a prerecorded briefing...

633
01:55:39,347 --> 01:55:41,439
...made prior to your departure...

634
01:55:42,917 --> 01:55:46,647
...and which for security reasons
of the highest importance...

635
01:55:47,955 --> 01:55:50,720
...has been known on board
during the mission...

636
01:55:51,725 --> 01:55:54,354
...only by your HAL-9000 computer.

637
01:55:57,865 --> 01:56:00,196
Now that you are in Jupiters space...

638
01:56:00,968 --> 01:56:03,163
...and the entire crew is revived...

639
01:56:03,571 --> 01:56:05,266
...it can be told to you.

640
01:56:08,176 --> 01:56:09,734
Eighteen months ago...

641
01:56:11,678 --> 01:56:15,240
...the first evidence
of intelligent life off the Earth...

642
01:56:15,917 --> 01:56:17,315
...was discovered.

643
01:56:19,220 --> 01:56:22,154
It was buried 40 feet
below the lunar surface...

644
01:56:23,024 --> 01:56:24,821
...near the crater "Tyco".

645
01:56:27,995 --> 01:56:30,760
Except for a single very powerful...

646
01:56:31,466 --> 01:56:34,435
...radio emission aimed at Jupiter...

647
01:56:36,738 --> 01:56:41,175
...the four-million-year-old black monolith
has remained completely inert.

648
01:56:43,610 --> 01:56:46,101
Its origin and purpose...

649
01:56:47,115 --> 01:56:48,980
...are still a total mystery.
